  "slug": "mutational-multimodal-36bc",
  "summary": "Predictive sequence models are now linking multiple mutation classes to variant-level assessment in cancer and inherited disease.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the joint modelling of mutation-generating processes and variant consequences at sequence resolution. MOTET jointly infers multimodal mutational processes across SBS, DBS and indel signatures in 2,583 PCAWG cancer genomes, while EAGLE-MUT, a DNA language model trained on cancer mutation data, predicts somatic mutation probabilities that vary with sequence context and nucleosome positioning. A monoallelic MUTYH carrier shows the oxidative SBS18 signature across multiple normal human tissues, suggesting a single hypomorphic allele. Variant-interpretation work applies computational assessment to chromosome 21 base-editing accessibility and to TP63-IRF6-GRHL3 coding and non-coding variants. A CTSC structural study links predicted destabilization to ClinVar pathogenicity labels. What is new is the combination of several mutation classes and predictive sequence models with variant-level assessment. The items differ in scope and maturity, and all six are preprints, so this thread is dominant rather than shared by every item.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
